From 09085a823d8d70bb2d608277ff114da054f993d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (for Kush)" <kushisthecoolest@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 13:27:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Linux: runtime and bridge names, FD interop import,
 headless present

- NativeExtractor maps Linux x86_64 to native/remix-sdk-linux64. A
  complete SDK there is marked by libremix.so; Windows keeps d3d9.dll.
  RemixRenderer resolves remix-sdk/libremix.so on Linux. RemixAPI loads
  System.mapLibraryName("remixbridge") from beside the runtime.
- gl/InteropHandles is the only place that knows how interop handles
  reach GL: Win32 handles with EXT_*_win32 (unchanged), or opaque file
  descriptors with EXT_memory_object_fd / EXT_semaphore_fd. A GL import
  takes ownership of its descriptor, so a dup() is imported and the
  runtime keeps its own; the class documents that assumption for the
  interop work. GlInterop and the extension check go through it.
- LinuxPresent: the Linux runtime is headless. There is no HWND, the
  framebuffer size goes to the runtime (remixlinux_SetWindowSize) before
  Startup and after every present, Present runs with
  skipVulkanSwapchain, and GLFW swaps the composited GL back buffer,
  also when the Remix side of the frame failed.
- Bridge natives linuxSetOutputSize, posixDupFd and posixCloseFd.
  On Windows they fail or do nothing.

Windows behaviour is unchanged on every path.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tb41TgDi74v7sgH5LJBZ5A
